      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "This is the \"r combinations from n\" problem.  To figure out how many different ways he can select 3 marbles out of 7, we can use the binomial coefficient, $\\binom{n}{r}$, which read \"n choose r\".  Plugging in 7 for $n$ and 3 for $r$: $\\binom{7}{3}$.  Recalling that the binomial coefficient is equal to $\\frac{n!}{r!(n-r)!}$, we have $\\frac{7!}{(3!)(4!)}$.  Evaluating this expression by multiplying, we find it to be: $\\frac{7\\times6\\times5}{3\\times2\\times1} = 35$.  Therefore, Johnny can make 35 different choices of 3 marbles to play his game. The final answer is: 35",
